Other names which aren't really 'official':
-------------------------------------------

Along with the 'official' Linux device names, there are some other names
commonly found in the /dev directory which may help folks find devices whose
names aren't immediately obvious.  These may be created either in the same
way the normal device files are created, or they may simply be links to the
'official' devices.  I personally recommend using symbolic links.

/dev/console -> /dev/tty0	(seems logical)

/dev/modem -> /dev/cuaS0	(or whichever is your modem)

/dev/mouse -> /dev/<whichever>	(I think "mgr" needs this one)

/dev/tty[a-d] -> /dev/ttyS[0-3] (to be compatable with some other unices)

/dev/COM[1-4] -> /dev/ttyS[0-3]	(for those with DOS hangups)

/dev/fd -> /proc/self/fd	(These support the "fd" ["File Descriptor"?]
/dev/stdin -> /dev/fd/0		 device through a symbolic link to the
/dev/stdout -> /dev/fd/1	 /proc filesystem.)
/dev/stderr -> /dev/fd/2

/dev/rmt* -> <tape drive>	(again, just to be nice)

That's it for this release of the Linux Device List.  Happy hacking!

